# The other half of resolution: an ingress that accepts a Moshpit name as a
# Host header.
#
# The resolvers answer `scrambled.eggs` with the gateway's address, and the
# browser then connects there sending `Host: scrambled.eggs`. Today that lands
# on Railway's edge, which only knows the domains registered against the
# service and answers `{"status":"error","code":404,"message":"Application not
# found"}` for everything else — and there is no way to register a domain per
# Moshpit name, because the whole point is that anyone can invent one.
#
# So the ingress has to be something that accepts *any* Host. Run this on the
# same box as a resolver (it already needs a static address), then point the
# resolvers at it:
#
#   MOSHPIT_GATEWAY_A=<this box's address>
#
# Until the hosting grid (PRD 0002 R7) exists to proxy into, this serves the
# clearnet preview page for the name — PRD 0004 R11's "extension-less preview
# fallback", which beats a 404 from a CDN nobody can debug.

{
	# Certificates for `scrambled.eggs` are not obtainable from a public CA: no
	# CA recognises a namespace it cannot validate against the ICANN root. So
	# port 80 is served plainly and HTTPS is left to clients that opted into
	# the namespace some other way (the extension, or a Moshpit CA when there
	# is one). Redirecting to HTTPS here would break every visitor this exists
	# to serve.
	auto_https off
}

:80 {
	# The gateway's own name still behaves normally.
	@self host pit.moshcode.sh dns.pit.moshcode.sh
	handle @self {
		redir https://pit.moshcode.sh{uri} permanent
	}

	# Any other Host is a Moshpit name that resolved here because it has no
	# site of its own yet. Send it to the pit with the name filled in: whoever
	# typed `mosh.whatever` has just demonstrated demand for that name, and the
	# pit can offer them the ending it sits under.
	#
	# A redirect rather than a proxy, deliberately. Proxying would leave people
	# signing in under a hostname no certificate authority will vouch for, with
	# a session cookie on a domain the app does not own. The redirect puts them
	# on the real origin, with a real padlock, where signing in works.
	handle {
		redir https://app.moshcode.sh/pit?name={host} 302
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}
}
